From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: damnbitimcrit <333091414+damnbitimcrit@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [APU] Exact audio pump interval; bound the output queue

The output queue, and with it the audio delay, grew by about one frame
every 80 seconds (about 0.75 frames a minute, +176 ms after 40 minutes,
up to the 64-frame cap of 341 ms). The pump interval is 5333 us, but a
256-sample frame at 48 kHz is 5333.33 us: the game was asked for frames
62.5 ppm faster than they play.

- apu_exact_pump_interval (default on): every third interval is 5334 us,
  16000 us per three frames, exact.
- apu_target_queued_frames (default 9): the level catch-up refills the
  queue to, instead of apu_prebuffer_frames (3). The fast cadence used
  to build a cushion by accident (up to 33 frames), and that cushion
  carried map loads, where the game's audio callbacks take 17-44 ms (up
  to 8.3 frames); loads that started with 3-9 frames queued ran dry
  about twice. With the exact interval nothing else fills the queue, so
  catch-up keeps 9 frames (48 ms) instead.
- apu_max_queued_frames (default 8): while the driver reports more than
  apu_target_queued_frames plus this many frames queued, a regular request
  is skipped and its output slot given back, so device clock drift can't
  grow the delay either. Only with a driver that reports its queue (XAudio2
  with apu_prebuffer_frames); 0 disables it.
- XAudio2's QueuedFrames no longer takes the driver's lock (the held count
  is atomic), so XAudio2's thread failing its try-lock again means a frame
  is being submitted.
- The skips are counted in the Halo 3 audio line.
---
